      "finding": "Pooled over holdout points the mean NIS is 155.9 (1500-point budget) / 127.2 (full reference), but the MEDIAN is 0.111 / 0.033. Restricting to thin (single-surface) neighbourhoods gives 0.0375 / 0.0121, while the mixed neighbourhoods alone give 234.0 / 206.5. Only 33% / 37% of neighbourhoods are thin, and the mean is set entirely by the rest. GT poses are therefore accurate: the score is manufactured by 8-NN plane fits that span two surfaces (the 8th neighbour sits ~2.1 m away in a 6 m corridor). Raising the reference budget improves the thin fraction and the thin-only NIS but cannot move the mean, because the estimator truncates every reference to 600 points.",
      "candidate_calibration": "DIAGNOSTIC ONLY - default values of compute_disjoint_holdout_point_to_plane_nis are deliberately left unchanged because they also drive already-published real-sequence holdout numbers. Candidate calibration to evaluate: (a) weight or reject neighbourhoods by planarity (drop the k-NN fit where the smallest eigenvalue exceeds ~0.05 x the middle one, i.e. where two surfaces mix); (b) report NIS as a robust quantile instead of a mean so mixed-plane frames do not dominate; (c) raise sigma to the actual surface sampling error measured on the analytic arena (~0.01 m) or, for real sequences, calibrate sigma per sensor; (d) scale the reference budget with the estimator cap rather than above it. Each of these changes reported numbers and needs the owner's sign-off."
